/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> techMovingAccentsLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Once accents can sit anywhere in the beat, your hands stop thinking in \"right hand on the beat\". Displaced accents are what make funk hi-hats, fusion fills and groups of three over four sound the way they do. They also test your time: the accent moves, the pulse must not.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Same four strokes as in Down & Up Strokes: the accent is a down stroke, the tap just before it is an up stroke, everything else is low taps. Keep the sixteenths alternating R L R L no matter where the accent is, so the accent changes hands by itself: on 1 and & it is the right hand, on e and a the left.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Tap your foot on the quarter notes, or count \"1 e & a\" out loud, never lose the beat. Line 6 accents every third note: the accent starts on 1, then lands on a, then &, then e, and comes home on beat 1 of bar 4. Line 8 is the 3-3-2 phrase you hear in countless grooves and fills.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "Aja (Steely Dan): Steve Gadd's drum break, accents all over the sixteenth grid. Funk hi-hat parts where the weight sits on the \"e\" or the \"a\". Rock fills phrased in threes across the bar line.",
  ),
];
